<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mahabharata</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 400 BCE–400 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mahabharata is one of the two great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> epics of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, traditionally attributed to the sage <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vyasa">Vyasa</a> and composed over several centuries between roughly 400 BCE and 400 CE. Its central story is the war at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurukshetra">Kurukshetra</a> between the Pandava and Kaurava branches of the Kuru dynasty. With about 100,000 verses it is among the longest poems ever written, and it contains the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhagavad-gita">Bhagavad Gita</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mahabharata.jpg" alt="Mahabharata" width="320" height="223" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kurukshetra.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Tradition ascribes the Mahabharata to the sage <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vyasa">Vyasa</a>, who is also a major figure in the epic and who described it as itihasa, history. Scholars regard the story of the scribe <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganesha">Ganesha</a> writing down Vyasa's dictation as a later interpolation, and the Critical Edition does not include it. The epic began as an orally transmitted tale of charioteer bards. Unlike the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a>, which had to be preserved letter-perfect, it was a popular work whose reciters conformed to changes in language and style, so its earliest surviving components are believed to be no older than the earliest external references to it, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panini">Panini</a>'s grammar of the 4th century BCE. The text probably reached its final form by the early Gupta period.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The core story is a dynastic struggle for the throne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hastinapura">Hastinapura</a>, ruled by the Kuru clan, between two collateral branches, the Kauravas and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandavas">Pandavas</a>. The eldest Kaurava, Duryodhana, is younger than the eldest Pandava, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yudhishthira">Yudhishthira</a>, and both claim to be first in line. The Pandavas build the capital of Indraprastha, but Duryodhana's uncle Shakuni wins their wealth and kingdom from Yudhishthira with loaded dice. They spend thirteen years in exile, and when negotiations through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> fail, both sides summon vast armies to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurukshetra">Kurukshetra</a>.</p><p>The epic runs to over 100,000 shlokas, about 1.8 million words, roughly ten times the length of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iliad">Iliad</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odyssey">Odyssey</a> combined. It is divided into 18 parvas and holds many other stories, including those of Damayanti, Shakuntala and Savitri and Satyavan, as well as discussions of philosophy and of dharmayuddha, just war, which sets criteria such as proportionality, just means and fair treatment of captives and the wounded. Its best-known part, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhagavad-gita">Bhagavad Gita</a>, is a dialogue between the Pandava prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arjuna">Arjuna</a> and his charioteer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Within the Indian tradition the Mahabharata is sometimes called the fifth Veda. Between 1919 and 1966 scholars at the Bhandarkar Oriental Research Institute in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pune">Pune</a> compared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and abroad and produced the Critical Edition, in 19 volumes on 13,000 pages, which is the text usually used in current studies. The epic was translated into Tamil and Javanese, where part of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-javanese">Kawi</a> prose version survives from the late 10th century. It inspired plays by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhasa">Bhasa</a> and many later works, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garhwal">Garhwal</a> region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttarakhand">Uttarakhand</a> villagers still perform the Pandav Lila, a ritual re-enactment of episodes from it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swami-vivekananda">Swami Vivekananda</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bal-gangadhar-tilak">Bal Gangadhar Tilak</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mahatma Gandhi</a>, among others, drew on the text in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-independence">Indian independence</a> movement.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mahabharata” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its court patronised literature in Bengali as well as Persian: Maladhar Basu composed the Sri Krishna Vijaya under Rukunuddin Barbak Shah, Krittibas Ojha produced the most popular Bengali Ramayana in this era, and the governor of Chittagong under Husain Shah sponsored a Bengali rendering of the <b>Mahabharata</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bengal-sultanate">Bengal Sultanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Atharvaveda treats the Magadhas as outsiders to Vedic orthodoxy, and the <b>Mahabharata</b> preserves legends of an earlier Brihadratha dynasty and its king Jarasandha.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-magadha">Kingdom of Magadha · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Dharmawangsa ordered the <b>Mahabharata</b> translated into Old Javanese in 996.</blockquote><a href="/empire/medang-kingdom">Medang Kingdom · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The great epics, the <b>Mahabharata</b> and the Ramayana, told the stories of gods and heroes; law books defined dharma, the duties of each class and stage of life; and devotion to personal gods, above all Vishnu and Shiva, grew alongside the older rites.</blockquote><a href="/religion/hinduism">Hinduism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The two great epics, the <b>Mahabharata</b> and the Ramayana, took shape over many centuries, roughly between 400 BCE and 400 CE.</blockquote><a href="/language/sanskrit">Sanskrit · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>The 33 surviving poems of the Paripatal praise Vishnu, Durga and Murugan, and the Kalittokai mentions Krishna, Shiva, Murugan, the Pandava brothers of the <b>Mahabharata</b> and the goddess Ganga.</blockquote><a href="/culture/sangam-age-tamilakam">Sangam-age Tamilakam · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li></ul></section>
